@meta
id: 10-07
title: Taking the quotes with it
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-quote, inner-vs-around
par: 33
minutes: 5
vscode: select from before the opening quote to after the closing one
open: CHANGELOG.md

@brief
`a"` is the string plus its quotes plus, like `aw`, one space. It is the
object you want when the string itself should stop existing — not be
replaced, removed.

That is rarer than `ci"` but it is the only tidy way to do it, because the
alternative is deleting the content, then the two quotes, then the space,
and hoping you counted.

The changelog entry for 2.4.0 mentions a flag that was removed before the
release went out, so the line is now wrong. Delete the quoted flag name
from that line so it reads as a sentence again. Then fix the last line:
the environment name is read from `SENTRY_ENV`, not `NODE_ENV`.

Do not touch the heading or the first bullet.

@keys
| `a"` | the string, its quotes, and one space |
| `da"` | delete all of that |
| `ci"` | change what is inside — still the common one |

@hints
One of these two edits removes a string, the other replaces one. They are
different objects.
--
`da"` takes the quotes and the space with it. `ci"` keeps the quotes and
replaces what is between them.
--
`f"` lands you on the opening quote, which counts as being on the string.
Then `da"` on the middle line and `ci"` on the last one.

@solution
    5j f" da"
    j f" ci" SENTRY_ENV <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Twenty-eight keystrokes. Look at the middle line afterwards: one space
between `The` and `flag`, not two. `da"` took the trailing space because
there was one.

That fallback rule is the thing to remember, and it bites in code. In
prose a string usually has a space after it, so `da"` reads your mind. In
TypeScript a string is usually followed by a comma, a bracket or a
semicolon and there is no trailing space at all — so `a"` falls back to
the space in *front* of it and you get `send(customer.email,'order-shipped'`
collapsed into `send(customer.email,`. Correct by vim's rule, wrong by
Prettier's.

So: `ci"` everywhere, `da"` in prose and in lists, and check the line
afterwards the first few times.

@start CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## 2.4.0

- Refunds now respect the "refund window" and stop after 30 days.
- The "detectOpenHandles" flag has been removed from the test scripts.
- Sentry reads the environment name from "NODE_ENV".

@expected CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## 2.4.0

- Refunds now respect the "refund window" and stop after 30 days.
- The flag has been removed from the test scripts.
- Sentry reads the environment name from "SENTRY_ENV".
